import SingleTestPage from '../components/bench/SingleTestPage.astro';
---

<SingleTestPage
  kind="gpu"
  title="GPU Benchmark Online: WebGPU GFLOPS Test — 9bench"
  description="Free online GPU benchmark: measures your graphics card's compute throughput in GFLOPS with a WebGPU shader, right in the browser. No download, nothing uploaded."
  kicker="GPU"
  h1="GPU benchmark online, in GFLOPS."
  lede="Runs a matrix-multiplication compute shader on your graphics card through WebGPU and reports the throughput in GFLOPS — billions of floating-point operations per second. A few seconds, no download, no account."
  duration="~2–6 seconds"
  runNote="Needs WebGPU: Chrome or Edge 113+, Firefox 147+, Safari 26+. Many Android phones have it switched off — then the test says “not measured” instead of showing a number."
>
  <section class="st__section">
    <h2 class="st__h2">What this GPU test measures</h2>
    <p class="st__p">
      The shader multiplies two 1024 × 1024 matrices of 32-bit floats. One pass is
      <code>2 × 1024³</code> ≈ 2.1 billion floating-point operations. The test warms the GPU
      up first, so a laptop GPU that idles at low clocks gets a fair reading, then takes three
      timed measurements and reports the middle one (the median), so a single lucky or
      throttled run cannot move the result. All three runs are shown next to it. GFLOPS is
      operations divided by time.
    </p>
    <p class="st__p">
      The adapter name comes from the browser. Some browsers hide it for privacy — the test
      still runs, it just cannot tell you which GPU it ran on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="st__section">
    <h2 class="st__h2">How the GPU score is calculated</h2>
    <code class="st__formula">GPU score = round( GFLOPS × 3 )</code>
    <p class="st__p">
      The same formula as in the full 9bench test. Calibration from the
      <a href="/methodology/#formulas">methodology</a>: a typical 2024 mid-range GPU reaches
      around 500 GFLOPS in the browser and scores about 1500.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="st__section">
    <h2 class="st__h2">Why GFLOPS here are far below the spec sheet</h2>
    <p class="st__p">
      Spec sheets list the theoretical FP32 peak. This test is a plain, untiled matrix
      multiplication: it is limited by cache and memory bandwidth long before the shader
      cores run out of arithmetic. In our own measurement an RTX 4060 Laptop reached
      372.8 GFLOPS — 2.9% of its theoretical peak. That is expected, not a fault: it is a
      throughput probe that ranks GPUs against each other, not a peak-FLOPS measurement.
    </p>
    <p class="st__p">
      Against Geekbench 7 on two laptops, the GPU ranking agreed: both put the same machine
      ahead. Our gap between them was wider. The full comparison is in the
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="st__p">Things that change the result:</p>
    <ul class="st__list">
      <li>Which GPU the browser picks. Laptops with integrated and dedicated graphics often run the browser on the integrated one unless you change it in the graphics settings.</li>
      <li>Battery vs. mains power.</li>
      <li>Browser and driver version.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="st__p">
      More on the unit itself: <a href="/articles/what-is-gflops-gpu-performance-explained/">what GFLOPS mean for GPU performance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</SingleTestPage>
